{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>The people spread about.</b> From the root of משוט בארץ (roaming the land), and this indicates that it did not fall one upon another, but scattered and dispersed." "<b>In mills.</b> Hand mills, and it is not possible to separate this name, for the mills would be two." "<b>In a mortar.</b> Wood or stone in which they pound; also possible that they would make cakes from what is pounded." "<b>Cakes.</b> As in ועשי עוגות (and make cakes)." "<b>Leshad.</b> Some say the lamed is added, like the lamed of לאנן (fresh) and לשלו (to him), and the word לשד ought to have the stress on the last syllable [milra] because it is an attribute of a noun [i.e., an adjective describing שמן]. But the correct view in my eyes is that the lamed of לשד is a root letter, as in נהפך לשדי, and it is the opposite of בחרבוני קיץ, and it is the heavy moisture that rises above all oil, and the word שמן will have the stress on the first syllable [mileil], according to its meaning."], :book :numbers, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {4 "The reference to נהפך לשדי (Lamentations 4:7) and בחרבוני קיץ (Jeremiah 48:11) is literal; both are understood here as forms demonstrating לשד as a root meaning clarified/cleared richness. The milra/mileil technical terms are retained exactly as in the source."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:17:14.621174039Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}